  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>

  <div class="faq-item"><h3>Q1. Is a higher flash point always safer for aerosols?</h3><p>Yes. A higher flash point reduces the likelihood of ignitable vapor formation at ambient temperatures.</p></div>
  <div class="faq-item"><h3>Q2. Can flash point be calculated instead of tested?</h3><p>No. Regulatory compliance requires laboratory-tested flash point values.</p></div>
  <div class="faq-item"><h3>Q3. Does propellant choice affect flash point?</h3><p>Yes. Hydrocarbon propellants drastically reduce overall flammability.</p></div>
  <div class="faq-item"><h3>Q4. Is flash point alone sufficient for aerosol classification?</h3><p>No. Additional spray ignition and pressure tests are required.</p></div>
  <div class="faq-item"><h3>Q5. Are water-based aerosols always non-flammable?</h3><p>Not necessarily. Alcohols and flammable propellants can still classify them as flammable.</p></div>

  

